This project uses **{{ARCHITECTURE_PATTERN}}**, with **{{UI_FRAMEWORK}}** and
**{{NAVIGATION_APPROACH}}** navigation. Every feature `/new-feature` generates
follows this pattern — no mixing patterns feature-to-feature (§5).

## Layer shape for {{ARCHITECTURE_PATTERN}}

<!-- /start keeps only the block matching Q5's answer. -->

### MVC
`Features/<Name>/<Name>ViewController.swift` (+ `.xib`/storyboard if used). Models
live in `Models/`, never in the feature folder. Simplest, most
Massive-View-Controller risk — reasonable only for very small apps. Fully
templated for UIKit (`mvc-uikit-coordinator/`) — deliberately one file, protocol
and networking implementation included, making the pattern's own risk honest
rather than hidden behind an extra layer. No SwiftUI variant: a "SwiftUI MVC"
has no real controller layer to template and would just duplicate the MV
pattern below.

### MVVM *(default)*
`Features/<Name>/{<Name>View.swift, <Name>ViewModel.swift, <Name>Repository.swift,
<Name>Service.swift}`. View binds to an `ObservableObject`/`@Observable`
ViewModel; ViewModel depends on the Repository's protocol directly — no
separate business-logic layer sits between them. (An earlier revision of this
template inserted a `UseCase.swift` here, matching Clean-Architecture-style
Android/Flutter conventions. It was dropped: for a single repository call with
no business rule of its own, it was a pure pass-through — see
`docs/CODING_STANDARDS.md`'s protocol-boundary rules. If a feature later needs
to orchestrate more than one repository/service, or apply a rule that belongs
in neither the ViewModel nor the Repository, add an `Interactor` — the native
iOS name for that layer — rather than reintroducing `UseCase`.)

```
SwiftUI View → ViewModel (published UI state, depends on Repository's protocol)
    → Repository → { Service (remote) | local persistence } → Models/ (shared)
```
State flows back up the same chain via `@Published`/`@Observable` — no second call
path, no layer-skipping.

### VIP (Clean Swift)
`Features/<Name>/{<Name>View.swift, <Name>Interactor.swift, <Name>Presenter.swift,
<Name>Router.swift, <Name>Worker.swift}` — plus, on SwiftUI only, **`<Name>ViewModel.swift`**.
Request/Response/ViewState structs live in `Models/<Name>Models.swift`, not
here. Strict unidirectional flow: View → Interactor → Presenter → View.

Fully templated for both UI frameworks:
- **UIKit** (`vip-uikit-coordinator/`) — classic Clean Swift. The ViewController
  conforms to `<Name>DisplayLogic` directly; the Presenter holds
  `weak var displayLogic: <Name>DisplayLogic?` pointing at it. A strong reference
  here leaks the whole scene on every push — this is the single most important
  correctness property in VIP, check it on every scene.
- **SwiftUI** (`vip-swiftui-navigationstack/`) — a SwiftUI `View` is a value type
  and can't hold a `weak` reference, so a thin `@Observable` ViewModel bridges
  the gap: View → **ViewModel** (conforms to `DisplayLogic`) → Interactor →
  Presenter (holds `weak displayLogic` pointing at the ViewModel) → Worker. The
  ViewModel contains no formatting and no business logic — it only holds
  published state and forwards intents to the Interactor.

**Don't confuse `<Name>Router.swift` above with the app-wide navigation
mechanism below.** This per-scene Router decides *where* this scene goes next
and hands that decision to whichever app-wide Coordinator/Router owns the
actual `UINavigationController`/`NavigationPath` — it never owns navigation
state itself.

### VIPER
`Features/<Name>/{<Name>View.swift, <Name>Interactor.swift, <Name>Presenter.swift,
<Name>Router.swift}`. Entity structs live in `Models/<Name>Models.swift`. Full
separation, heaviest boilerplate. Differs from VIP only by dropping the `weak`
reference and giving Router full navigation ownership — close enough to VIP
that it's deliberately template-assisted rather than given its own template;
write its layer bodies from VIP's shape above with that one difference.

### MV (SwiftUI-native)
`Features/<Name>/{<Name>View.swift, <Name>Model.swift}`. `<Name>Model` is
presentation state (an `@Observable` class calling services), not a data model —
data models still live in `Models/`. Fewest layers, least isolatable for unit
testing.

## Two fixed rules, regardless of pattern (§3.2)

- A leaf feature's folder is **flat** — no `Presentation/`/`Domain/`/`Data/`
  subfolders inside `Features/<Name>/`. Grouping folders *above* a leaf feature
  are fine (`Features/Settings/Profile/`).
- Models never live inside a feature folder. Every model type — shared domain
  model or single-feature Request/Response/Entity struct alike — lives in the
  top-level `Models/` package, filed as `Models/<Feature>Models.swift`.

## Navigation: {{NAVIGATION_APPROACH}}

<!-- /start keeps only the block matching Q6's answer. -->

### `UINavigationController` + Coordinator
The Coordinator holds the `UINavigationController` and calls
`pushViewController`; SwiftUI screens are wrapped in `UIHostingController` before
pushing. Works regardless of whether individual screens are UIKit or SwiftUI.
{{#if UI_FRAMEWORK_IS_SWIFTUI}}This app hosts every screen via `UIHostingController`
and pushes/pops through the Coordinator — never a SwiftUI `NavigationLink` (§2.3:
mixing both mechanisms in one app is what actually breaks).{{/if}}

### SwiftUI `NavigationStack` + Router
A `Router` holds a `NavigationPath`/typed `Route` array; Views append/remove from
it via `.navigationDestination(for:)`. Less glue code, fully declarative.

Whichever is chosen is the **one** navigation mechanism for the whole app —
`/new-feature` always wires into it, never introduces a second path (§3.4).

{{#if HAS_TABS}}
## Tab-based navigation

Each tab owns its own navigation stack — never a single shared one. Pushing a
detail screen from one tab never affects another tab's back stack (§3.5).
{{/if}}

## Shared states

Every list/detail screen consumes `LoadingView`, `ErrorView` (message + optional
retry), and `EmptyStateView` from `DesignSystem/SharedViews/` — never a
per-feature reimplementation (§3.6). This applies to SwiftUI screens directly;
the UIKit combos (VIP+UIKit, MVC+UIKit) render the equivalent loading/error/empty
states as plain UIKit views owned by the ViewController itself rather than a
shared SwiftUI component — extract a shared UIKit equivalent once a second
UIKit feature needs the same states, not before (§8.2: don't abstract on
speculation).

## Icons and images

Prefer SF Symbols (`Image(systemName:)`/`UIImage(systemName:)`) over an
imported icon asset wherever a suitable symbol exists — reserve
`Assets.xcassets` for genuinely custom iconography a symbol can't express.
Remote images load via `AsyncImage` by default; upgrade to a caching library
(Nuke, Kingfisher) only once a project's image volume or caching needs justify
the dependency — see `docs/ai/ui_rules.md`.
